      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is part 3 of 3 of a certificate — a fragment. The fragment contains:\n\n1. A table with environmental classes, ambient temperature range, and power supply voltage (characteristics)\n2. Certificate header info: NMi, OIML Member State Netherlands, Number R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02 revision 3, Project number 3800938, Page 8 of 9\n3. Table 7: Software versions of the 5700 electronics — Transmitter Software (W&M), Internal Core Processor, PIC Firmware, LCD PIC Firmware with checksums\n4. Notes (footnotes) explaining the software table markers\n5. Page 9 of 9: Certificate history table with revisions\n\nLet me structure this per the schema:\n\n**certificate:**\n- number: \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02\"\n- revision mentioned — the schema doesn't have revision at cert level but there's revision_history\n- scheme: \"A\" (from the number R117/2007-A)\n- project_number: \"3800938\"\n- page_total: 9\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- The date_issued isn't explicitly stated, but revision 3 is dated 11 September 2024 per the history table. The certificate date issued might be that. Hmm — the fragment shows \"revision 3\" in the number, and the revision history shows revision 3 dated 11 September 2024. I could infer date_issued: 2024-09-11. But rule 8 says don't fabricate. The revision history table is present in the fragment, so it's reasonable to infer that revision 3 (the current one) was issued 11 September 2024. I think it's safe to include date_issued: \"2024-09-11\" with a note, or maybe just leave it. Actually since revision 3 is the current revision and its date is 11 September 2024, this is a reasonable inference. Let me include it.\n\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present in fragment (NMi's issuer ID might be elsewhere). Omit.\n\n**issuing_authority:**\n- name: \"NMi\" (NMi Certin B.V. typically, but fragment just says \"NMi\"). I'll use \"NMi\" since that's what's in the fragment.\n- Other fields not present.\n\n**recommendation:**\n- id: \"R117\" (canonical, not \"OIML R 117\")\n- edition: the number says R117/2007, so edition 2007. Edition is int: 2007.\n\n**characteristics:**\n- type_level:\n  - environmental_classes: {value: \"M3 / E3 / H3\"} — hmm, this is a list of classes. Value as string \"M3/E3/H3\". Actually per R60/R76 classification: M3, E3, H3 are environmental/classification classes. Value: \"M3/E3/H3\" as string.\n  - ambient_temperature_range: two conditions — depends on display configuration. This is config_level:\n    - condition: \"display is the primary indication\" → {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n    - condition: \"approved external display is used as primary indication\" → {min: -40, max: 55}, unit °C\n  - power_supply_voltage: two options: 21–90 VDC and 100–240 VAC. This could be config_level with axis \"power_supply_type\":\n    - VDC: 21–90 V\n    - VAC: 100–240 V, 50–60 Hz\n  - power_supply_frequency: 50–60 Hz (for AC)\n\nLet me use config_level for these:\n\n```json\n\"config_level\": [\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"ambient_temperature_range\",\n    \"axis\": \"display_configuration\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"display is the primary indication\", \"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"approved external display is used as primary indication\", \"value\": {\"min\": -40, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"power_supply_voltage\",\n    \"axis\": \"power_supply_type\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"DC\", \"value\": {\"min\": 21, \"max\": 90}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"AC\", \"value\": {\"min\": 100, \"max\": 240}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"power_supply_frequency\",\n    \"axis\": \"power_supply_type\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"AC\", \"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"environmental_classes\",\n    ... hmm\n  }\n]\n```\n\nEnvironmental classes: \"M3 / E3 / H3\" — this isn't really a config axis; it's a set of classes the instrument complies with. I'll put in type_level as value \"M3/E3/H3\" string. Actually wait — for a mass flow meter (this is a Micro Motion CMF sensor cert — CMF010, CMF025, CMF050, CMFHC, MVD800, 5700 transmitter), these classes: M3 (electromagnetic environment?), E3 (climatic?), H3 (mechanical hazard)? Actually in WELMEC/OIML classification: E = climatic, M = electromagnetic, H = mechanical. So M3/E3/H3 are environmental classifications. Keep as string value.\n\n**matrix_tables:**\nTable 7 — Software versions. This is a multi-column table with version/checksum pairs. It has sections: Transmitter Software (W&M), Internal Core Processor, PIC Firmware, LCD PIC Firmware.\n\nI could represent this as matrix_tables with name \"Software versions of the 5700 electronics\". Columns would be... the table has pairs of version/checksum columns repeated 3 times, with section headers. Rather than trying to preserve the exact 3-column-pair layout, I could restructure rows as software_component + version + checksum. The schema says matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables. Let me flatten:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Software versions of the 5700 electronics\",\n  \"columns\": [\"software_component\", \"version\", \"checksum\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"software_component\": \"transmitter_software_weights_and_measures\", \"version\": \"1.20\", \"wm_version\": \"1.0\", \"checksum\": \"2DF0D8E9\"},\n    ...\n  ],\n  \"description\": \"...\",\n  \"footnotes\": [\"*)\", \"**)\"]\n}\n```\n\nHmm, the transmitter software versions are like \"1.20 (1.0)\" where the parenthetical is the W&M software version. Per note *), the transmitter software and W&M software form a matched set; W&M software doesn't have a checksum.\n\nLet me list the transmitter software entries:\n- 1.20 (1.0) → 2DF0D8E9\n- 1.30 (1.1) → ADE631BB\n- 1.85 (2.0) ETO28130 → 0EA71B41\n- 2.00 (2.0) → 2F52132D\n- 2.10 (2.0) → 23DD3385\n- 3.0 (3.0) → 06108400\n- 3.1 (3.0) → 2DE64BB2\n- 3.2 (3.0) → 8CB1FE4B\n- 4.0 (3.0) → 0E4997D5\n- 4.07 (4.0) → 44477758\n- 4.1 (4.0) → AFE0673B\n- 4.2 (4.0) → 627B3E99\n- 4.30 (4.0) → AC509A54\n\nInternal Core Processor:\n- 4.02 → 8D61C368\n- 4.14 → 40860C63\n- 4.20 → 2983A9BE\n- 4.40 → B280233F\n- 4.42 → D7BA0841\n- 4.50 → 6B48C624\n- 4.60 → DDB76E3C\n- 4.70 → AEB92E3F\n- 4.80 → F1583A44\n- 4.90 → 6083BF9B\n- 5.08 → 4D368E71\n- 5.10 → 82C541D9\n- 5.20 → BD69FDD6\n- 5.22 → F4A8D922\n- 5.30 → 65828884\n- 5.33 → BF3164F6\n- 5.40 → 0218C30B\n\nPIC Firmware:\n- 8.0 → 0000DE9C\n\nLCD PIC Firmware:\n- 3.0 → 000081D5 (for transmitter 1.20)\n- 3.0 → 00007442 (for 1.30 and later)\n\nAlso note \"ETO28130\" appears alongside 1.85 (2.0) — that's probably an order code/variant. I'll keep it in the row as a note or extra field.\n\nI'll structure matrix_table rows with fields: section, version, wm_version, checksum, notes.\n\nFor LCD PIC: version 3.0, checksum 000081D5 with condition \"transmitter software 1.20\" and checksum 00007442 with condition \"transmitter software 1.30 and later\".\n\n**revision_history:**\n- Initial, 30 September 2021, \"-\" → changes: maybe \"Initial issue\" or null. The description is \"-\", so changes could be \"Initial issue\" — but don't fabricate; description is \"-\". I could put changes: \"-\" or null. Schema revision (string). Let me use:\n  - {revision: \"Initial\", date: \"2021-09-30\", changes: null} — hmm, \"-\" means no description. Maybe \"Initial issue\". I'll put changes: \"Initial issue\"? That's mild fabrication. Better: changes: \"-\"? Or null. I'll use null and maybe note. Actually the revision is \"Initial\" with description \"-\". I'll set changes: null.\n  \n  Actually, to be safe and faithful: changes: \"-\". Hmm, the schema says changes. Empty strings are not values — use null or omit. So null is better.\n\n- {revision: \"1\", date: \"2022-11-14\", changes: \"Addition of liquefied gases