I also include model_family? The fragment mentions models: MVD800 Enhanced Core Processor, 5700 internal core processor, CMF010, CMF025, CMF050, CMFHC sensors. But this is a fragment — part 3 of 3. The instruction says \"conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only. Omit sections that are not present in this fragment.\" The fragment mentions these model identifiers within revision history text. Should I add certified_type.type_designations? The fragment does name components: \"5700 electronics\" (Table 7 title), \"MVD800 Enhanced Core Processor\", measurement sensors CMF010/CMF025/CMF050/CMFHC. These appear in revision history text, not as formal declarations in this fragment. I think it's reasonable to omit model_family/certified_type since this fragment doesn't formally declare them. But the \"5700 electronics\" is the subject of Table 7. Hmm.\n\nThe fragment is one certificate but with pages 8-9. The first table (environmental classes) lacks a page header but belongs to the same cert presumably. Since instruction says extract what's present in this fragment, I'll focus on: characteristics table, software matrix table, footnotes, revision history, and certificate metadata.\n\nI might add components[] for the 5700 electronics and MVD800? The revision 3 mentions \"MVD800 Enhanced Core Processor or 5700 internal core processor\" — these are core processor options. Given the fragment includes Table 7 \"Software versions of the 5700 electronics\", I could add components entries:\n- role: \"transmitter/electronics\", type_designations: [\"5700\"]\n- Also MVD800? It appears only in revision text. Hmm.\n\nI think a light touch: include components for \"5700\" transmitter (with software info) since Table 7 is explicitly about it. And maybe MVD800 mentioned. Actually, to avoid over-reach, I'll include components:\n\n```json\n\"components\": [\n  {\"role\": \"transmitter_electronics\", \"type_designations\": [\"5700\"]},\n  {\"role\": \"core_processor\", \"type_designations\": [\"MVD800 Enhanced Core Processor\"], \"alternatives\": \"OR\"},\n  ...\n]\n```\n\nHmm, this is getting speculative. The MVD800 vs 5700 internal core processor are alternatives (\"or\"). Let me include minimal components:\n\n- {\"role\": \"transmitter\", \"type_designations\": [\"5700\"], characteristics: {software: ...}} — but characteristics within components isn't in schema shape exactly; components have characteristics{} free-form.\n\nActually the schema components entry: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. I can put software versions in components[0].characteristics, but I already capture them in matrix_tables. Simpler: keep components minimal with just role/type_designations, and the software table in matrix_tables.\n\nLet me decide: include components with:\n1. {\"role\": \"transmitter electronics\", \"type_designations\": [\"5700\"]}\n2. {\"role\": \"enhanced core processor\", \"type_designations\": [\"MVD800\"]}\n\nAnd note alternatives OR? The MVD800 or 5700 internal core processor appear as alternatives for the Qmin lowering. I'll add \"alternatives\": \"OR\" on a combined entry? They're different roles though. I'll list them separately without alternatives, or one entry: {\"role\": \"core processor\", \"type_designations\": [\"MVD800 Enhanced Core Processor\", \"5700 internal core processor\"], \"alternatives\": \"OR\"}. That seems reasonable.\n\nHmm, but is this over-extraction from revision history prose? The instruction says extract content present in this fragment. These are named. I'll include them lightly.\n\nNow the first table — environmental classes etc. Where does it belong? characteristics.type_level or config_level. There's no page header before it, but it's presumably page 7 or 8 content of the same cert. I'll include as characteristics.\n\nambient_temperature_range is config-dependent → config_level with axis \"display_configuration\" / \"primary_indication\".\n\npower_supply: two