\"note\"].\n\ndescription: \"Software versions of the 5700 electronics. The W&M software version is given in parentheses next to the transmitter software version.\"\nfootnotes: [\"*)\", \"**)\"] — in matrix_tables footnotes, keep markers.\n\nNow check numeric parsing: checksums are strings (hex) — fine as strings. Versions are strings like \"4.02\" — as strings (they're version identifiers, not numbers).\n\nrevision_history dates: \"30 September 2021\" → \"2021-09-30\", \"14 November 2022\" → \"2022-11-14\", \"23 November 2022\" → \"2022-11-23\", \"11 September 2024\" → \"2024-09-11\". I'll use ISO dates. Or keep original strings? Schema just says date. ISO is cleaner. I'll use ISO.\n\ncertificate.date_issued: \"2024-09-11\" — from revision 3 being current. Include with confidence.\n\nrecommendation: id \"R117\", edition 2007, scheme \"A\". accuracy_classes: not in fragment — omit.\n\ntest_reports: none in fragment — omit.\n\nissuing_authority: name \"NMi\". The header shows \"NMi\" as logo text. Include name: \"NMi\". Address unknown — omit.\n\nmember_state: \"The Netherlands\".\n\nAlso \"page 8 of 9\" and \"page 9 of 9\" — page_total: 9.\n\nNow compose the JSON. Keys ordering per schema.\n\nOne question: certificate.number — should it include \"revision 3\"? The number as printed: \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02 revision 3\". I'll put number: \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02\" and the revision is reflected in revision_history (latest revision 3). Maybe also keep number as full string? I think splitting is cleaner: number \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02\". The revision info is in history.\n\nEnvironmental classes value — hmm, should this go under type_level with value string \"M3/E3/H3\"? These correspond to R76/2014 classes (climatic E3? actually for automatic weighing... but this is a flow meter R117; still environmental classifications). Keep string.\n\nLet me also double check \"Ambient temperature range\" values: \"-25…+55 °C\" → min -25 max 55, \"°C\". \"-40…+55 °C\" → min -40 max 55.\n\nPower: \"21… 90 VDC\" → 21–90 V, DC. \"100…240 VAC, 50…60 Hz\" → 100–240 V AC, 50–60 Hz.\n\nconfig_level entries:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"ambient_temperature_range\",\n  \"axis\": \"primary_indication_display\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"the display is the primary indication\", \"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"an approved external display is used as primary indication\", \"value\": {\"min\": -40, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nSchema says config values: {condition, value}. Should unit_symbol be allowed in values? Schema example in rule 5: \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}.\" Yes.\n\nPower supply:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"power_supply_voltage\",\n  \"axis\": \"power_supply_type\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"DC\", \"value\": {\"min\": 21, \"max\": 90}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"AC\", \"value\": {\"min\": 100, \"max\": 240}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"}\n  ]\n},\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"power_supply_frequency\",\n  \"axis\": \"power_supply_type\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"AC\", \"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\ntype_level:\n```json\n\"environmental_classes\": {\"value\": \"M3/E3/H3\", \"unit_symbol\": null}\n```\n\nNow assemble everything. Should I include \"certified_type\"? Not declared in fragment — omit per instructions.\n\nComponents: I'll include:\n\n```json\n\"components\": [\n  {\"role\": \"transmitter electronics\", \"type_designations\": [\"5700\"]},\n  {\"role\": \"core processor\", \"type_designations\": [\"MVD800 Enhanced Core Processor\", \"5700 internal core processor\"], \"alternatives\": \"OR\"}\n]\n```\n\nHmm — \"5700 internal core processor\" — the 5700 transmitter has an internal core processor (the \"Internal Core Processor\" section of Table 7 is within 5700). The MVD800 is the \"Enhanced Core Processor\"